description:
Implement the bounded first slice of the operational-workflow architecture:

- add a soft phase-transition readiness gate to coordination-protocol;
- distinguish output acceptance from readiness for the next phase;
- define fuzzy agent-evaluated trigger conditions and human-owned material decisions;
- remove retro from the global skill manifest after the explicit command replacement exists;
- preserve existing coordination-protocol edits and preserve slice-retro unchanged;
- deploy and verify generated targets.

Parent architecture work remains open; this task does not consolidate supervisor, wrap, slice-supervisor or other operational skills.

Problem

Tracker-specific guidance currently contains lifecycle policy. tools/br/SKILL.md has a Required Workflow that moves from ready and claim through work to close. That policy does not belong to br: the same operational lifecycle must work with any tracker, and closure may require verification, independent review and human adjudication outside tracker semantics.

Evidence

Chemify Stage 4 was closed by an executor after embedding review and decision claims in its own report. The br sequence ending in close conflicted with the phase-transition gates and made executor closure appear normal. Independent recovery later showed that content was strong but review chronology and candidate adjudication were not durably established.

Boundary

The br skill should own only safe use of beads_rust:
- command and storage semantics that prevent incorrect tracker operations;
- auto-export behaviour;
- cross-tracker routing;
- JSON-shape and silent-failure gotchas;
- lock/daemon/tool-specific behaviour.

It must not own:
- when work is ready;
- who claims work;
- execution, verification or review sequence;
- who may close;
- phase transition or handoff policy.

The cheatsheet may document command shapes such as br close without prescribing when lifecycle policy permits closure.

Acceptance criteria

- Remove tracker-independent lifecycle instructions from the br skill and cheatsheet.
- Point to the tracker-agnostic operational protocol only where needed.
- Confirm descriptions and triggers still route br command questions correctly.
- Deploy and verify generated targets.
- Check other tracker-specific skills for the same policy leak without widening this task into the full operational-architecture redesign.

Related: parent skills-ems.

## Brief

Run a read-only quality and stale-reference audit of the key/global skills,
using the existing facet audit as context. Concentrate first on Git/workflow,
ticketing, deployment, search routing, and the largest skills.

## Look for

- missing or misleading descriptions and triggers;
- oversized skills whose reference material should move to cheatsheets;
- broken absolute paths and missing referenced files;
- deprecated or dead mechanisms still reachable from active guidance;
- duplicated or contradictory policy;
- provenance gaps in vendored skills;
- documentation counts and phase notes that no longer match the tree.

## Acceptance criteria

1. Findings are reported as evidence-backed items with path and line context.
2. Each finding is classified as fix, consolidate, retire, project-scope, or
   monitor.
3. Existing tickets are mapped to findings; no duplicate ticket is created
   merely because an old ticket is not yet closed.
4. The report proposes a small next batch of safe, independently verifiable
   changes.
